      "judge_rationale": "The candidate summary accurately captures the chapter’s main claims, examples, and evidence: the traditional (callous) model of toughness, Bobby Knight’s success and abusive methods, Baumrind’s responsiveness/demandingness framework and the authoritarian style (including the 31% stat), the harms (Jordan McNair, rhabdo, psychological outcomes), the contrast between fake and real toughness, Pete Carroll and other humanistic coaches, and the 2008 Eastern Washington study. These central concepts and most qualifiers (e.g., that punishment isn’t always bad, the Goldilocks balance of high demand + high support, that real toughness is navigating discomfort rather than bulldozing) are preserved and clearly presented.\n\nDeductions: the summary introduces elements that are not supported by the provided source text or the supplied rubric excerpt. The detailed “Author’s Journey” section (Don Bowden Mile, Magness, paradoxical vocal cord dysfunction) and specific four-pillar labels are not present in the supplied source/rubric and therefore reduce faithfulness. There is also a slight framing imprecision when the summary states “This style is prevalent; 81% of Americans thought parents were too soft,” which conflates a public-perception statistic with prevalence of authoritarian parenting. These unsupported additions and the one minor mischaracterization justify lowering the faithfulness score while leaving high marks for coverage, preservation of qualifiers, concision, and organization.",
